      "level": 2,
      "content": "PART A: FOUNDATIONAL UNDERSTANDING OF INEQUALITY"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Inequality is multidimensional, spanning income, wealth, gender, social, and regional dimensions.",
        "Poverty is an absolute state of deprivation, whereas inequality measures relative resource distribution across a population.",
        "Wealth inequality is inherently more severe and persistent than income inequality due to asset accumulation over generations.",
        "The Lorenz Curve graphs cumulative income distribution, and the Gini Coefficient quantifies this inequality mathematically.",
        "Achieving balanced economic development requires targeted government interventions and inclusive growth strategies."
      ],
      "id": "hwspki0"
    },
